static void smk_cipso_doi(void);
doip->doi = smk_cipso_doi_value;
rc = netlbl_cfg_cipsov4_add(doip, &nai);
Reason:
The old smk_cipso_doi() function lacked synchronization and reused the global smk_cipso_doi_value directly. When a previously used DOI was written again, netlbl_cfg_cipsov4_add() failed with -EEXIST because the old DOI entry still existed in the NetLabel subsystem. Additionally, the default map was removed before adding the new DOI, so if the add failed, the system was left without an IPv4 default map, disabling networking for non‑ambient labels. Concurrent writes could also cause inconsistent states.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
static DEFINE_MUTEX(smk_cipso_doi_lock);
if (smk_cipso_doi_value == ndoi)
	goto clr_doi_lock;
if (smk_cipso_doi_value != CIPSO_V4_DOI_UNKNOWN) {
	rc = netlbl_cfg_map_del(NULL, PF_INET, NULL, NULL, &nai);
	if (rc && rc != -ENOENT)
		goto clr_ndoi_def;
	netlbl_cfg_cipsov4_del(smk_cipso_doi_value, &nai);
}
Reason:
A new mutex serializes writes to /smack/doi. If the new DOI equals the current value, the function returns early without any change. Before adding a new DOI, the old DOI and its default map are explicitly removed (only if a DOI was previously configured). The new DOI is added first, and only after success does the old map get deleted, ensuring the system never loses its default map. This prevents the -EEXIST error and guarantees consistent network label mappings.

